{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"dockerImageVersionId":31400,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Why We Need Statistics in Data Science\n\n*A hands-on walk-through, built from Dr. Abhinanda Sarkar's classroom session (Great Learning).*\n\nData science and machine learning have been called the hardest job of the 21st\ncentury. Whatever the title, one thing is constant underneath all of it:\n**statistics is the language you use to reason about data you cannot fully see.**\n\nWith statistics we make claims like *\"sales will fall most in this customer\nsegment next quarter\"* or *\"there's a 16% chance this positive HIV test is a true\npositive.\"* That isn't magic and it isn't a crystal ball - it's just statistics.\n\nThis notebook is a guided tour. Every idea is paired with **runnable code** so you\ncan change a number and watch the idea respond. The arc is:\n\n1. **Two ways of thinking** - statistics vs. machine learning\n2. **Descriptive → Predictive → Prescriptive** - the three jobs of analytics\n3. **Descriptive statistics** - mean vs. median, spread, skew, outliers\n4. **Correlation & covariance** - how two variables move together\n5. **Linear regression as description** - summarizing many variables at once\n6. **Probability** - coping with uncertainty\n7. **Conditional probability & Bayes' theorem** - spam filters and medical tests\n8. **Distributions** - binomial, Poisson, and the normal distribution\n\nRun the next cell first - it sets up everything.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nnp.random.seed(42)          # so the synthetic data is reproducible\nplt.rcParams['figure.figsize'] = (8, 4)\nplt.rcParams['axes.grid'] = True\nprint(\"Environment ready.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:37.407148Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:37.408138Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:38.964246Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:37.408098Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:38.963158Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Environment ready.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1. Two ways of thinking: statistics vs. machine learning\n\nThere is a real cultural difference between the two communities:\n\n- The **statistical** way of thinking says: *first formulate a problem, then go\n  get the data to solve it.* Here **data is cheap and the question is expensive** -\n  you are paid for asking the right question.\n  \n- The **machine-learning** way of thinking says: *here is the data, tell me what it\n  is telling you.* Here **the question is cheap and the data is expensive** - you are\n  paid for collecting and exploiting the data.\n\nThe classic interview deadlock captures it:\n\n> **Business:** \"Here is my data. What can you say?\"\n> **Statistician:** \"What do you want to know?\"\n> **Business:** \"That's why I want to hire *you*.\"\n> **Statistician:** \"If you don't tell me what you want to know, how do I know what to tell you?\"\n\nNeither side is wrong. Real work lives in between. Take a concrete example: a watch\ncompany's sales are falling. You can't answer *\"why?\"* by staring at numbers - you\nhave to **frame questions first**: Which segments are declining? How fast? Where is\nthe bulk of sales? Only then does the data become useful.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2. Descriptive, Predictive, Prescriptive\n\nAnalytics work usually flows through three stages. The cricket analogy makes them\nclick instantly:\n\n| Stage | Question | Cricket example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Descriptive** | What happened? It locates and isolates the problem. | How many centuries has the batsman scored? (look it up) |\n| **Predictive** | What *will* happen, or what happens if I change something? | How many centuries will he score in the World Cup? |\n| **Prescriptive** | What should I *do* to change the outcome? | How do we get him to score *more* centuries? |\n\nA doctor does all three: *describe* your symptoms, *predict* you are becoming\npre-diabetic, then *prescribe* an action - under constraints (first, do no harm).\nA business does the same: sell more watches, but stay profitable, keep employees,\nrepay loans. Prescription is a constrained optimization problem.\n\n**This notebook focuses mostly on the descriptive foundation**, because everything\npredictive and prescriptive is built on top of it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3. A dataset to think with: CardioGoodFitness\n\nThe market-research team of a fitness company wants the **profile of the typical\ncustomer** for each treadmill product. They collect data on people who bought a\ntreadmill in the last three months.\n\nWe generate a synthetic version of the well-known *CardioGoodFitness* dataset so\nevery cell here runs with no external file. The columns mirror the real study:\n\n- `Product` - model bought (TM195, TM498, TM798)\n- `Gender`, `Age`, `Education` (years),\n- `MaritalStatus`\n- `Income` - annual household income\n- `Usage` - times per week the customer plans to use it\n- `Fitness` - self-rated 1 (poor) to 5 (excellent)\n- `Miles` - expected miles walked/run per week\n\nNotice that some of these are **measured facts** (age, income) and some are\n**opinion masquerading as data** (planned usage, self-rated fitness). It's still\ndata - it came from somewhere.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def make_cardio(n=180):\n    products = np.random.choice(['TM195', 'TM498', 'TM798'], size=n, p=[0.44, 0.33, 0.23])\n    gender = np.random.choice(['Male', 'Female'], size=n, p=[0.58, 0.42])\n    # Age: right-skewed (most customers young, a tail of older buyers)\n    age = (18 + np.random.gamma(shape=2.2, scale=4.8, size=n)).round().clip(18, 50).astype(int)\n    education = np.random.choice([12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20], size=n,\n                                 p=[0.05, 0.10, 0.22, 0.10, 0.40, 0.10, 0.03])\n    marital = np.random.choice(['Partnered', 'Single'], size=n, p=[0.59, 0.41])\n    # Fitness depends loosely on the product tier\n    tier = pd.Series(products).map({'TM195': 0, 'TM498': 1, 'TM798': 2}).values\n    fitness = np.clip(np.round(2.7 + 0.7 * tier + np.random.normal(0, 0.6, n)), 1, 5).astype(int)\n    usage = np.clip(np.round(2.5 + 0.8 * tier + np.random.normal(0, 0.9, n)), 2, 7).astype(int)\n    # Income: log-normal-ish, lifted by education and product tier -> mean > median\n    income = (np.random.lognormal(mean=10.7, sigma=0.28, size=n)\n              + 1500 * (education - 14) + 9000 * tier).round(-2).clip(29000, 105000).astype(int)\n    # Miles driven by usage and fitness, with noise\n    miles = np.clip(np.round(-56 + 20 * usage + 27 * fitness + np.random.normal(0, 18, n)),\n                    20, 360).astype(int)\n    return pd.DataFrame({\n        'Product': products, 'Age': age, 'Gender': gender, 'Education': education,\n        'MaritalStatus': marital, 'Usage': usage, 'Fitness': fitness,\n        'Income': income, 'Miles': miles,\n    })\n\ndata = make_cardio()\ndata.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:38.965843Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:38.966597Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.017216Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:38.966566Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.016334Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":3,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"  Product  Age  Gender  Education MaritalStatus  Usage  Fitness  Income  Miles\n0   TM195   42    Male         14     Partnered      3        3   52000    113\n1   TM798   21    Male         16     Partnered      4        5   91000    168\n2   TM498   26  Female         16     Partnered      4        5   51900    169\n3   TM498   31  Female         16        Single      2        3   53900     84\n4   TM195   29    Male         16     Partnered      4        2   40700     71","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Product</th>\n      <th>Age</th>\n      <th>Gender</th>\n      <th>Education</th>\n      <th>MaritalStatus</th>\n      <th>Usage</th>\n      <th>Fitness</th>\n      <th>Income</th>\n      <th>Miles</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>TM195</td>\n      <td>42</td>\n      <td>Male</td>\n      <td>14</td>\n      <td>Partnered</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>52000</td>\n      <td>113</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>TM798</td>\n      <td>21</td>\n      <td>Male</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>Partnered</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>91000</td>\n      <td>168</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>TM498</td>\n      <td>26</td>\n      <td>Female</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>Partnered</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>51900</td>\n      <td>169</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>TM498</td>\n      <td>31</td>\n      <td>Female</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>Single</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>53900</td>\n      <td>84</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>TM195</td>\n      <td>29</td>\n      <td>Male</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>Partnered</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>40700</td>\n      <td>71</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":3},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`head()` and `tail()` give a feel for the data - which variables exist and what\n*kind* each one is. `info()` reports the storage type: numeric columns vs. `object`\n(text/categorical) columns. This distinction matters: you cannot take the *mean* of\n`Gender`.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"data.info()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.018412Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.019094Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.03628Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.019064Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.035296Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"<class 'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame'>\nRangeIndex: 180 entries, 0 to 179\nData columns (total 9 columns):\n #   Column         Non-Null Count  Dtype \n---  ------         --------------  ----- \n 0   Product        180 non-null    object\n 1   Age            180 non-null    int64 \n 2   Gender         180 non-null    object\n 3   Education      180 non-null    int64 \n 4   MaritalStatus  180 non-null    object\n 5   Usage          180 non-null    int64 \n 6   Fitness        180 non-null    int64 \n 7   Income         180 non-null    int64 \n 8   Miles          180 non-null    int64 \ndtypes: int64(6), object(3)\nmemory usage: 12.8+ KB\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":4},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4. Describing one variable: location and spread\n\n`describe()` gives the **five-number summary** (min, 25%, 50%, 75%, max) plus the\nmean and standard deviation for every numeric column. For categorical columns it\ncan only count and report the most frequent value - it literally cannot compute a\nmean.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"data.describe()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.038816Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.039288Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.068379Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.039247Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.067525Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":5,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"              Age   Education       Usage     Fitness         Income  \\\ncount  180.000000  180.000000  180.000000  180.000000     180.000000   \nmean    28.527778   15.200000    3.211111    3.311111   54660.555556   \nstd      7.023846    1.722023    0.985872    0.800062   14238.320695   \nmin     19.000000   12.000000    2.000000    1.000000   29000.000000   \n25%     23.750000   14.000000    2.000000    3.000000   43825.000000   \n50%     27.000000   15.000000    3.000000    3.000000   53350.000000   \n75%     32.000000   16.000000    4.000000    4.000000   62575.000000   \nmax     50.000000   20.000000    6.000000    5.000000  105000.000000   \n\n            Miles  \ncount  180.000000  \nmean    98.827778  \nstd     39.390585  \nmin     20.000000  \n25%     70.000000  \n50%     96.000000  \n75%    128.250000  \nmax    206.000000  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Age</th>\n      <th>Education</th>\n      <th>Usage</th>\n      <th>Fitness</th>\n      <th>Income</th>\n      <th>Miles</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>count</th>\n      <td>180.000000</td>\n      <td>180.000000</td>\n      <td>180.000000</td>\n      <td>180.000