:"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"       Product Gender MaritalStatus\ncount      180    180           180\nunique       3      2             2\ntop      TM195   Male     Partnered\nfreq        88    100            99","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Product</th>\n      <th>Gender</th>\n      <th>MaritalStatus</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>count</th>\n      <td>180</td>\n      <td>180</td>\n      <td>180</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>unique</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>top</th>\n      <td>TM195</td>\n      <td>Male</td>\n      <td>Partnered</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>freq</th>\n      <td>88</td>\n      <td>100</td>\n      <td>99</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":6},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Mean vs. median - the most important pair of numbers\n\nAsk: *\"What is one representative age for this dataset?\"* There are two natural answers.\n\n- **Mean** = the *average age of a person*: add all ages, divide by how many.\n- **Median** = the *age of the average person*: sort everyone, pick the middle one.\n\nThey answer subtly different questions, and they react differently to extreme\nvalues. The median ignores outliers; the mean chases them.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"mean_age = data['Age'].mean()\nmedian_age = data['Age'].median()\nprint(f\"Mean age:   {mean_age:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Median age: {median_age:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Min / Max:  {data['Age'].min()} / {data['Age'].max()}\")\n\n# mean > median  =>  data is pushed to the right (right-skewed)\nprint(f\"\\nmean - median = {mean_age - median_age:.2f}  (positive -> right skew)\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.085687Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.086092Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.107582Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.08604Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.106592Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Mean age:   28.53\nMedian age: 27.00\nMin / Max:  19 / 50\n\nmean - median = 1.53  (positive -> right skew)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":7},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**The Ambani effect.** Imagine the richest person in the country walks into the\nroom. The *mean* income explodes; the *median* barely moves, because the middle\nperson is still the middle person. That is why the per-capita income of a country is\nalmost always higher than the income of the *typical* citizen. Let's prove it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"incomes = data['Income'].copy()\nprint(f\"Before - mean: {incomes.mean():,.0f}   median: {incomes.median():,.0f}\")\n\nincomes_with_billionaire = pd.concat([incomes, pd.Series([5_000_000])], ignore_index=True)\nprint(f\"After  - mean: {incomes_with_billionaire.mean():,.0f}   \"\n      f\"median: {incomes_with_billionaire.median():,.0f}\")\nprint(\"\\nThe mean jumps; the median is almost unchanged. The median is robust to outliers.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.108799Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.109594Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.193998Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.109553Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.193167Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Before - mean: 54,661   median: 53,350\nAfter  - mean: 81,983   median: 53,400\n\nThe mean jumps; the median is almost unchanged. The median is robust to outliers.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 5. Standard Deviation\n\n### The idea in one line\nStandard deviation measures **how spread out** numbers are around their average - the *typical distance from the middle*. Low = consistent and predictable. High = wildly spread out.\n\n### Why the average isn't enough\nTwo things can share the **same average** but feel totally different.\n\n**Pizza prices at two restaurants (both average `~$15`):**\n- **Restaurant A** - every pizza is around `$15`. *Low* standard deviation: consistent, no surprises.\n- **Restaurant B** - a $2 slice and a `$100` caviar pizza. *High* standard deviation: wild swings.\n\nThe average alone can't tell them apart. The standard deviation can.\n\n### How to calculate it (5 steps)\nExample - five ages: **20, 22, 24, 26, 28**\n\n1. **Mean:** (20+22+24+26+28) ÷ 5 = **24**\n2. **Distance from mean:** −4, −2, 0, +2, +4\n3. **Square each** (so minuses don't cancel pluses): 16, 4, 0, 4, 16\n4. **Average them** -> this is the **variance**: 40 ÷ 5 = **8**\n5. **Square root** (back to normal units): √8 ≈ **2.83**\n\nSo people sit about **2.8 years** from the average age, give or take. (Square root with the root = standard deviation; without it = variance.)\n\n### Real-life example: two coffee shops\nBoth shops have an average wait time of 5 minutes. So by the average alone, they look the same. But:\n\nShop A is consistent - you almost always wait about 5 minutes.\nShop B is chaotic - sometimes 1 minute, sometimes 12.\n\nStandard deviation is the number that exposes this difference. Let me run it so you can see real output.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import numpy as np\n\n# Wait times (minutes) over 10 visits to two coffee shops\nshop_A = [5, 4, 6, 5, 5, 4, 6, 5, 5, 5]    # steady\nshop_B = [1, 9, 2, 8, 5, 12, 3, 7, 1, 2]   # chaotic\n\nfor name, waits in [(\"Shop A (steady)\", shop_A), (\"Shop B (chaotic)\", shop_B)]:\n    waits = np.array(waits)\n    mean = waits.mean()\n    std = np.sqrt(((waits - mean) ** 2).mean())   # the 5 steps in one line\n    print(f\"{name}: average = {mean:.1f} min, std dev = {std:.2f} min\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.195309Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.195694Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.212801Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.195654Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.211712Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Shop A (steady): average = 5.0 min, std dev = 0.63 min\nShop B (chaotic): average = 5.0 min, std dev = 3.63 min\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Same average, very different consistency - and standard deviation is what reveals it.\n\n\n### When to use it\n- Measuring **consistency or risk** - wait times, scores, prices, returns.\n- Comparing groups with **similar averages** to see which is more stable.\n- Symmetric/bell-shaped data, where the **68–95–99.7 rule** holds (68% within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2, 99.7% within 3).\n\n**Avoid it when** data is **skewed or has big outliers** (e.g. incomes) — squaring lets one extreme value dominate. Use **median + IQR** instead.\n\n### Why data scientists use it\n\n**1. Spotting outliers & fraud**\n- Meaning: a value far from the average (more than ~3 SD away) is suspicious.\n- Example: a card normally spends `$50`; a sudden `$9,000` charge is way outside the spread → flagged as fraud.\n- Takeaway: SD draws the line between \"normal\" and \"weird.\"\n\n**2. Putting features on one scale (Z-score)**\n- Meaning: rescale every column with `z = (value − mean) / SD` so they sit on the same ruler.\n- Example: \"Age\" (0–100) and \"Income\" ($0–$100,000) - without scaling, the model over-weights income.\n- Takeaway: SD lets the model treat every variable fairly.\n\n**3. Checking if a model is reliable**\n- Meaning: look at the spread (SD) of the model's mistakes, not just the average error.\n- Example: a weather app off by \"5 min ± 1\" is trustworthy; off by \"5 min ± 40\" is useless.\n- Takeaway: low SD of errors = consistently accurate.\n\n**4. A/B testing - real win or luck?**\n- Meaning: SD sets the \"margin of error\" around each result.\n- Example: a new web page gets 12% clicks vs the old 10% - if that gap beats the SD, it's a real improvement.\n- Takeaway: SD tells you when a difference is trustworthy, not random.\n\n**5. Quick feel for new data (EDA)**\n- Meaning: a glance at each column's SD shows which ones vary a lot.\n- Example: \"Age\" SD = 7 (tight) vs \"Income\" SD = 14,000 (very spread) - income carries more signal.\n- Takeaway: high SD flags variables worth a closer look.\nWant me to drop this into your markdown guide file?","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"x = data['Age'].values\nmean = x.mean()\n\nvariance = np.mean((x - mean) ** 2)          # population variance (divide by n)\nstd_pop = np.sqrt(variance)\nstd_sample = x.std(ddof=1)                    # sample std (divide by n-1)\nmad = np.mean(np.abs(x - mean))               # mean absolute deviation (Laplace's version)\n\nprint(f\"Variance (1/n):              {variance:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Std dev (1/n):               {std_pop:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Std dev (1/(n-1), pandas):   {std_sample:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Mean absolute deviation:     {mad:.2f}\")\nprint(\"\\nStd dev is inflated by far-away points because distances are squared.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.214114Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.214467Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.234329Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.21443Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.233294Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Variance (1/n):              49.06\nStd dev (1/n):               7.00\nStd dev (1/(n-1), pandas):   7.02\nMean absolute deviation:     5.45\n\nStd dev is inflated by far-away points because distances are squared.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":10},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n**What is the mean?**\nThe mean is the **average age** = **28.5 years**. Add up all 180 customers' ages and divide by 180. It's the single \"center\" number that represents the typical customer.\n\n**What is the final standard deviation?**\nThe standard deviation is **7 years** (7.02 from pandas). This is the **typical distance** each customer's age sits away from that average of 28.5.\n\n**What does the result mean together?**\nPut the two numbers side by side - **28.5 ± 7** - and you get a quick portrait of your customers:\n- The center of the group is age **28.5**.\n- Most people are spread about **7 years** on either side, so the bulk of customers fall roughly between **21 and 36** years old.\n- A small standard deviation would mean everyone is nearly the same age; a large one would mean ages are all over the place. 7 years is a moderate spread.\n\n**What did we *understand* by calculating it?**\nThe mean alone is not enough. Two customer groups could both average 28.5, but one might be all 27–30 year-olds (tight) and another a mix of teenagers and 50-year-olds (wide). The standard deviation is the number that **tells these apart** - it measures how consistent or how varied the group is.\n\n**Why is this helpful?**\nWith just two numbers (28.5 and 7) you can describe 180 people at a glance - no need to read every row. That helps you make real decisions: who to design the product for, who to target in ads, and what age range to plan around. And later it powers bigger things - spotting unusual customers (outliers), comparing groups fairly, and checking whether a model's predictions are reliable.\n\nIn one sentence: **the mean tells you where your customers are centered, and the standard deviation tells you how spread out they are - together they summarize the whole group honestly.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 6. Seeing the shape: histogram and box plot\n\nA histogram bins the data and counts how many fall in each bin - it turns a column\nof numbers into a *picture* of the distribution. The box plot is a compact version\nof the five-number summary, and it flags outliers automatically.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 4))\nmean_age = data['Age'].mean()\nmedian_age = data['Age'].median()\ndata['Age'].hist(bins=15, ax=axes[0], color='lightblue', edgecolor='black')\naxes[0].axvline(mean_age, color='crimson', linestyle='--', label=f'mean {mean_age:.1f}')\naxes[0].axvline(median_age, color='green', linestyle='--', label=f'median {median_age:.1f}')\naxes[0].set_title('Histogram of Age (right-skewed)')\naxes[0].set_xlabel('Age'); axes[0].set_ylabel('Count'); axes[0].legend()\n\ndata.boxplot(column='Age', by='Gender', ax=axes[1])\naxes[1].set_title('Age by Gender (box plot)')\naxes[1].set_xlabel('Gender'); axes[1].set_ylabel('Age')\nplt.suptitle('')  # remove the automatic super-title\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.236774Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.237087Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.744265Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.237052Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.74295Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1200x400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":11},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Left chart - the histogram (the shape of all ages)\n\nThink of it as sorting every customer into age \"buckets\" and stacking them up:\n\n- Each **bar** is an age range. The **taller** the bar, the **more people** are that age.\n- You can see most bars are bunched on the **left** (the 20s). The tallest bar is around age 26 - that's the most common age.\n- As you move right (older ages), the bars get **short** - only a handful of customers are in their 40s and 50s.\n\nSo the story: **most customers are young (early-to-mid 20s), with a long thin tail of older folks stretching to the right.** That right-hand tail is what \"right-skewed\" means.\n\nThe two **dashed lines** show the two kinds of \"average\":\n- Green (median, 27) = the *middle* customer's age.\n- Red (mean, 28.5) = the *arithmetic average* age.\n\nThe red line sits a little **to the right** of the green line. That gap is the proof of the skew - those few older customers drag the average up, even though the typical person is 27. (This is exactly why median is often the more honest \"typical\" value.)\n\n### Right chart - the box plots (comparing men vs. women)\n\nSame age data, but now **split into two groups** so we can compare. Each box summarizes one group:\n\n- The **box** holds the **middle 50%** of that group (from the 25% mark to the 75% mark).\n- The **green line inside** the box is that group's **median** (typical age).\n- The **whiskers** (the vertical lines sticking out) reach the normal low and high ages.\n- The **little circles at the top** are **outliers** - unusually old customers who stand apart from the rest.\n\nWhat it tells us: **men and women have almost the same age profile** - both medians sit around 26–27, both boxes cover roughly 23–32. The main difference is the **male group has more older outliers** (those circles climbing toward 50).\n\n### The big picture - why bother?\n\nWe're doing a quick \"**who are our customers?**\" health check before any serious analysis. In two glances we now know:\n\n1. Our typical buyer is in their **mid-to-late 20s**.\n2. The data **leans young** with a few older buyers (right skew).\n3. **Gender barely changes the age picture** - so we probably don't need separate age strategies for men and women.\n\nThat's the whole point: a couple of pictures reveal the shape, the center, and the oddballs in the data **faster and more clearly than any table of numbers could.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 7. Percentile , Quartiles, IQR (Interquartile Range)\n\n### 1. Percentile - \"what % is below me?\"\n\nA percentile answers: *out of everyone, what fraction scored lower than this point?*\n\nIn your data, **Income** ranges from about `$30,000` to `$105,000`. If we say *\"the 75th percentile of income is `$62,575` ,\"* it means **75% of your customers earn less than $62,575**, and only the top 25% earn more.\n\nReal-life feel: it's the same idea as a school result - *\"you're in the 90th percentile\"* means you beat 90% of students. Or a baby's growth chart - *\"50th percentile for height\"* means exactly average height for that age.\n\n### 2. Quartiles - slicing the data into 4 equal groups\n\nQuartiles are just three special percentiles that cut your sorted data into **four equal quarters**:\n\n- **Q1 = 25th percentile** -> for Age, that's **~24**. A quarter of customers are younger than 24.\n- **Q2 = 50th percentile = median** -> Age **27**. The exact middle customer.\n- **Q3 = 75th percentile** -> Age **32**. Three-quarters are younger than 32.\n\nSo your customers split like this by age:\n```\nyoungest 25%  |  next 25%  |  next 25%  |  oldest 25%\n   19–24      |   24–27    |   27–32    |    32–50\n            (Q1)       (median)      (Q3)\n```\n\n### 3. IQR - the spread of the \"middle crowd\"\n\n**IQR = Q3 − Q1 = 32 − 24 = 8 years.**\n\nIt's the age range covered by the **middle 50%** of customers - ignoring the youngest quarter and the oldest quarter. It tells you how tightly the *typical* group is packed, **without letting the extremes interfere.**\n\n### Why use them - grounded in your data\n\n**Reason 1: they don't get fooled by outliers.**\nLook at your Income column - most people earn `$40k`–`$60k`, but a few earn `$84,300`, `$91,000`, even `$105,000`. Those few high earners drag the **mean** income up. The **median** (`$53,350`) and the quartiles barely move, because they only care about *who's in the middle of the line*, not how rich the richest person is. So for income, the median + IQR tell the honest \"typical customer\" story.\n\n**Reason 2: they tell you \"where do I stand?\"**\nCustomer #1 earns `$52,000`. On its own that number means little. But knowing it's near the **median** instantly tells you he's a perfectly average earner — not rich, not poor. That ranking is the useful part.\n\n**Reason 3: IQR is a clean, outlier-proof measure of spread.**\nStandard deviation squares distances, so one `$105,000` earner inflates it. IQR uses only the middle 50%, so those extremes can't distort it.\n\n**Reason 4: they power automatic outlier detection.**\nUsing the **fence rule** on Age: lower fence = Q1 − 1.5×IQR = 24 − 12 = 12; upper fence = Q3 + 1.5×IQR = 32 + 12 = 44. Any customer older than 44 gets flagged - and indeed your 45-to-50-year-olds (like customer #0 at 42 is fine, but a 50-year-old) are the outliers. This is exactly how the dots appear on a box plot.\n\n### When to check / where to use (in this dataset)\n\n| In your data | Use this | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Income, Miles** (skewed - long tail of high values) | **Median + IQR** | A few big earners / high-mileage users would distort mean + std |\n| **Fitness, Education** (symmetric, ratings 1–5) | **Mean + std dev** | Balanced, so the mean is honest |\n| Ranking a customer (\"top 10% earners to target premium model\") | **Percentile** | Directly gives position |\n| Cleaning data before modeling (\"flag weird ages/incomes\") | **IQR fences** | Robust, standard anomaly rule |\n| Drawing a **box plot** of Age by Gender | **Quartiles + IQR** | The box = Q1–Q3, whiskers = the fences |\n\n### Your one rule of thumb\n\n> **Symmetric, clean data -> mean + standard deviation.**\n> **Skewed data or outliers -> median + IQR + percentiles.**\n\nIn your treadmill data, **Age and especially Income lean right** (a few older, higher-earning customers), so the **median and IQR describe your typical customer more truthfully** than mean ± std. That's why both toolsets exist - you pick the one that won't lie about *your* particular column.\n\nWant me to write the code that builds a full quartile + IQR + outlier summary for **every numeric column** in this dataset (Age, Income, Miles, etc.), so you can see it all at once?","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"q1, q3 = np.percentile(x, [25, 75])\niqr = q3 - q1\nlower_fence = q1 - 1.5 * iqr\nupper_fence = q3 + 1.5 * iqr\noutliers = x[(x < lower_fence) | (x > upper_fence)]\n\nprint(f\"Q1 = {q1:.1f},  Q3 = {q3:.1f},  IQR = {iqr:.1f}\")\nprint(f\"Whisker fences: [{lower_fence:.1f}, {upper_fence:.1f}]\")\nprint(f\"Outliers flagged: {sorted(outliers.tolist())}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.745844Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.746282Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.756054Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.746238Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.755114Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Q1 = 23.8,  Q3 = 32.0,  IQR = 8.2\nWhisker fences: [11.4, 44.4]\nOutliers flagged: [45, 46, 47, 48, 49, 50, 50, 50]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":12},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Skewness - when the mean and median disagree\n\nWhen a distribution is **symmetric**, mean = median. When the mean sits to the\n*right* of the median, data has been pushed right -> **right-skewed**. A simple\nskewness measure is `mean - median`. Let's check every numeric column.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"num_cols = ['Age', 'Education', 'Usage', 'Fitness', 'Income', 'Miles']\nsummary = pd.DataFrame({\n    'mean': data[num_cols].mean(),\n    'median': data[num_cols].median(),\n    'std': data[num_cols].std(),\n    'skew (mean-median)': data[num_cols].mean() - data[num_cols].median(),\n    'pandas skew': data[num_cols].skew(),\n})\nsummary.round(2)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.757339Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.757703Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.791446Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.757665Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-05T18:53:39.790278Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":13,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"               mean   median       std  skew (mean-median)  pandas skew\nAge           28.53     27.0      7.02                1.53         1.18\nEducation     15.20     15.0      1.72                0.20         0.22\nUsage          3.21      3.0      0.99                0.21         0.55\nFitness        3.31      3.0      0.80                0.31         0.17\nIncome     54660.56  53350.0  14238.32             1310.56         0.61\nMiles         98.83     96.0     39.39                2.83         0.21","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>mean</th>\n      <th>median</th>\n      <th>std</th>\n      <th>skew (mean-median)</th>\n      <th>pandas skew</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Age</th>\n      <td>28.53</td>\n      <td>27.0</td>\n      <td>7.02</td>\n      <td>1.53</td>\n      <td>1.18</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Education</th>\n      <td>15.20</td>\n      <td>15.0</td>\n      <td>1.72</td>\n      <td>0.20</td>\n      <td>0.22</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Usage</th>\n      <td>3.21</td>\n      <td>3.0</td>\n      <td>0.99</td>\n      <td>0.21</td>\n      <td>0.55</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Fitness</th>\n      <td>3.31</td>\n      <td>3.0</td>\n      <td>0.80</td>\n      <td>0.31</td>\n      <td>0.17</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Income</th>\n      <td>54660.56</td>\n      <td>53350.0</td>\n      <td>14238.32</td>\n      <td>1310.56</td>\n      <td>0.61</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Miles</th>\n      <td>98.83</td>\n      <td>96.0</td>\n      <td>39.39</td>\n      <td>2.83</td>\n      <td>0.21</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":13},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 8. Covariance and Correlation\n\nSo far, one variable at a time (**univariate**). Now we ask how two variables move\ntogether (**bivariate**). The building block is, for each point, the product\n\n$$(x_i - \\bar{x})(y_i - \\bar{y}).$$\n\n- Both above their averages, or both below -> the product is **positive**.\n- One above, one below -> the product is **negative**.\n\nAverage those products and you get the **covariance**. Its *sign* tells you the\ndirection of the relationship. Its *magnitude*, unfortunately, depends on the units\n(measure height in mm vs. m and the number changes).","metadata":{}}]}